WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.498
A common mistake is skipping analysis.

2
00:00:02.498 --> 00:00:07.797
When teams assume they already know the problem, they often build the wrong course.

3
00:00:07.797 --> 00:00:21.727
The result may explain information learners already understand, ignore the real performance barrier, or force eLearning into a problem better solved by a job aid, process change, coaching, or clearer communication.

4
00:00:21.727 --> 00:00:24.528
Another mistake is using vague objectives.

5
00:00:24.528 --> 00:00:32.704
Objectives such as know, understand, or be aware can be useful as informal language, but they rarely guide design by themselves.

6
00:00:32.704 --> 00:00:42.621
If the objective does not identify what learners should do with the knowledge, the course may drift into content coverage rather than performance support.

7
00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:46.027
Copying source documents into slides is also common.

8
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:50.570
Source material is valuable, but it is not automatically instruction.

9
00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:59.124
Policies, manuals, and procedure documents often need translation into examples, decisions, scenarios, summaries, and practice.

10
00:00:59.124 --> 00:01:06.089
The course should help learners use the source material, not simply reproduce it in a less convenient format.

11
00:01:06.089 --> 00:01:10.707
Interactions and media can become mistakes when added without purpose.

12
00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:15.854
A click-to-reveal interaction that hides ordinary text may slow learners down.

13
00:01:15.854 --> 00:01:19.639
A video that could have been a simple diagram may waste time.

14
00:01:19.639 --> 00:01:23.046
A quiz that asks trivia may not measure the objective.

15
00:01:23.046 --> 00:01:27.361
Every course element should have an instructional reason to exist.

16
00:01:27.361 --> 00:01:34.402
Finally, teams sometimes treat LMS upload as the end of implementation and completion as the only evaluation.

17
00:01:34.402 --> 00:01:40.533
Real implementation includes launch testing, communication, access, support, and tracking.

18
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:45.151
Real evaluation asks whether the course improved the intended outcome.

19
00:01:45.151 --> 00:01:51.889
ADDIE becomes bureaucracy only when teams follow the labels without using them to make better decisions.
